 :doc/body "# OpenRouter provider application — Murakumo\n\nStatus: DRAFT / NOT SUBMITTED. This replaces the September 1 application narrative, not the independent Basho/Hokusai product roadmap. Do not send until the gates below are evidenced.\n\n## Proposed offering\nMurakumo proposes a self-hosted upstream text model, qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70, served through https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions. It is not an original model or a proprietary fine-tune. The advertised alias murakumo-main is unsuitable for this application because its response can identify a different backend. Basho's current upstream relay and the untrained Hokusai artifact are not part of this proposal.\n\nCandidate catalog: https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/openrouter/models. This separate projection uses the official ModelDocumentV2 schema version 2.4 and deliberately reports is_ready=false. It makes no ZDR, certified-compliance, dedicated-capacity or proprietary-training claim. The existing /v1/models remains the product discovery surface, not the application catalog.\n\n## Evidence as of 2026-09-08\nTwo initial public requests to the fixed candidate returned HTTP 200, the requested model ID, correct changing marker text, stop, and consistent positive prompt/completion usage. The streaming request also supplied [DONE]. These are low-volume protocol checks, not a model-weight attestation, uptime history or a load test.\nThe candidate catalog is checked against a vendored official schema, including negative cases. The smoke host rejects wrong models, missing usage, incomplete streams and oversized responses. It writes timestamps, model identity, timing, token counts and pass/fail, not customer prompts or generated content.\n\n## Qualification plan\nRun scripts/api-smoke.cljs --provider-sample every 15 minutes, making one non-streaming and one streaming request sequentially, with a 45-second timeout. Append results to ~/.murakumo/openrouter-b70-samples.jsonl. Report with --provider-report.\nOur internal low-volume observation gate is seven days, at least 1,000 non-429 requests (at least 500 in each mode), at least 99.5% correctness success excluding HTTP 429, no observation gap over 45 minutes, successful-request p95 completion <=10 seconds and streaming TTFT <=5 seconds. HTTP 429 is reported separately, never erased. These are internal targets, not OpenRouter's published acceptance criteria. A rolling window permits recovery from old failures; sleep or a stopped scheduler creates a coverage gap.\nEven when this observation gate passes, production-ready remains false. A small repeated prompt cannot establish long-context quality, parallel-load capacity, billing correctness or outage recovery.\n\n## Remaining submission gates\n1. Pin the exact upstream repository, revision and weight hashes, quantization and commercial serving license. The returned model ID alone is insufficient evidence. Proposed context/output limits require a boundary test against that artifact.\n2. Exercise long prompts, concurrent requests, cancellation, queue overflow, 429/Retry-After and backend restart recovery. Reserve capacity so existing workloads cannot consume the promised offer. Establish supported request parameters with measured results.\n3. Establish the OpenRouter credential, authorization and per-provider accounting boundary. Current anonymous public smoke success does not prove authenticated partner isolation or billable metering.\n4. Publish approved, final privacy/retention and terms pages covering edge, inference origin, asynchronous storage and operational logs. Current public legal pages are marked DRAFT; no whole-service ZDR claim is justified. Public URLs: https://murakumo.cloud/legal-privacy and https://murakumo.cloud/legal-terms.\n5. Confirm the contracting entity, billing contact, monthly invoice and reconciliation procedure. Do not infer legal or invoice readiness from a company name on a website.\n6. Finish seven-day observation and attach the raw ledger hash, UTC interval, request counts, 429 count, failure causes, throughput and latency percentiles. Cross-check public catalog against actual deployment.\n7. Rewrite the final submission from verified evidence, then submit and retain an external receipt. A draft, merged PR, deployed endpoint or scheduler is not an application receipt.\n\n## Sources\nhttps://openrouter.ai/providers/apply\nhttps://openrouter.ai/docs/assets/provider-monitor-schema-v2.openapi.json\nPrevious original-model plan: adr-2609011842-awai-japanese-models-openrouter-provider-application.\n"}]
